I investigated this and traced down the exact root cause in Boost Math and SciPy.

### Root Cause Analysis

In `scipy/special/boost_special_functions.h`, `ncf_sf_wrap` calls:
```cpp
template<typename Real>
Real
ncf_sf_wrap(const Real x, const Real v1, const Real v2, const Real l)
{
    return boost::math::cdf(boost::math::complement(
        boost::math::non_central_f_distribution<Real, StatsPolicy>(v1, v2, l), x));
}
```

In Boost Math's `boost/math/distributions/non_central_f.hpp`:
```cpp
template <class RealType, class Policy>
RealType cdf(const complemented2_type<non_central_f_distribution<RealType, Policy>, RealType>& c)
{
    ...
    r = detail::non_central_beta_cdf(x, cx, alpha, beta,
        c.dist.non_centrality(), true, Policy());
    return r;
}
```
Notice that it calls `detail::non_central_beta_cdf(..., invert=true)`.

Now look at `detail::non_central_beta_cdf` in `boost/math/distributions/non_central_beta.hpp`:
```cpp
if(l == 0)
    result = cdf(boost::math::beta_distribution<RealType, Policy>(a, b), x);
else if(x > cross)
{
    result = detail::non_central_beta_q(..., static_cast<value_type>(invert ? 0 : -1));
    invert = !invert;
}
else
{
    result = detail::non_central_beta_p(..., static_cast<value_type>(invert ? -1 : 0));
}
if(invert)
    result = -result;
```
For `l != 0`, `non_central_beta_p` / `non_central_beta_q` computes an offset $\pm 1$ such that when `invert` is true, the final `result = -result` flips it to $1 - P = Q$.

However, when `l == 0`, it computes `result = cdf(...)` (which is $P$), but **does not account for `invert == true`**. At the end of the function, `if (invert) result = -result;` unconditionally negates $P$, returning $-P$ instead of $1 - P$!

Hence, `scu._ncf_sf(x, dfn, dfd, 0.0)` always returns $-P = -\text{cdf}$ instead of $1 - P$.

### Proposed Fix

In `scipy/special/boost_special_functions.h`, `ncf_sf_wrap` can check `l == 0` and route to `fisher_f_distribution` (or return `cdf(complement(fisher_f_distribution(...), x))`):

```cpp
template<typename Real>
Real
ncf_sf_wrap(const Real x, const Real v1, const Real v2, const Real l)
{
    if (l == 0) {
        return boost::math::cdf(boost::math::complement(
            boost::math::fisher_f_distribution<Real, StatsPolicy>(v1, v2), x));
    }
    return boost::math::cdf(boost::math::complement(
        boost::math::non_central_f_distribution<Real, StatsPolicy>(v1, v2, l), x));
}
```

`boost/math/distributions.hpp` already includes `fisher_f_distribution`. This directly restores the intended mathematical identity $ncf(v_1, v_2, \lambda=0) \equiv F(v_1, v_2)$ for the survival function and ensures $0 \le sf \le 1$.

I'd be happy to prepare a PR with regression tests in `scipy/special/tests` and `scipy/stats/tests` if maintainers agree with this approach.

